// @vesper/chorus: Vesper port of the choreography front end of
// vesper-chorus (port wave P3b lane 6, #1068): parsing, well-formedness,
// endpoint projection with merging, binary duality and the bounded
// asynchronous explorer (design section 25.1, 25.5).
//
// All state lives in one handle `C`: the symbol table (util.ves), the
// global tree arena (tree.ves), the hash-consed local-type arena
// (local.ves), the projector (project.ves), the explorer (explore.ves) and
// the parser (parse.ves). The Rust wrapper (src/port.rs) encodes its
// inputs as words and text, calls one entry point below and rebuilds its
// own types from `out` and the symbol table.
//
// Input words. A string is two words, `(offset, length)` into the text
// argument. A choreography is `nroles, role strings.., ntree, tree..`; a
// tree is in postorder (children first):
//   End `5`; Var `4 s`; Rec `body.. 3 s`; Local `next.. 2 role s`;
//   Comm `next_1.. next_n 1 from to n (label payload)*n`.
// A local type is in postorder too:
//   End `8`; Var `7 s`; Rec `body.. 6 s`; Action `next.. 5 s`;
//   Send/Recv `next.. 1|2 peer label payload`;
//   Select/Offer `next_1.. next_n 3|4 peer n (label payload)*n`.
// Output words use the same forms with one word, a symbol id, per string.
use self::util::{
    inc,
    none,
    iw
};
use self::tree::{
    read_chor,
    read_roles,
    validate
};
use self::local::{
    are_dual,
    emit_l,
    l_init,
    read_local
};
use self::project::{
    project_role
};
use self::explore::{
    explore_run
};
use self::parse::{
    parse_src
};

